        "sourceId": 603,
        "text": "<|endoftext|> of foreign and Commonwealth Affairs to the Secretary of State.\n\n\"We condemn the attack on our embassy and consulate in Libya,\" White House Communications Director Anthony Scaramucci said in a statement. \"The United States will do all we can to protect Americans and underscore the importance of the safety and security of our people in the targeted countries.\"​<|endoftext|>Ready to fight back? Sign up for Take Action Now and get three actions in your inbox every week. You will receive occasional promotional offers for programs that support The Nation’s journalism. You can read our Privacy Policy here. Sign up for Take Action Now and get three actions in your inbox every week.\n\nThank you for signing up. For more from The Nation, check out our latest issue\n\nSubscribe now for as little as $2 a month!\n\nSupport Progressive Journalism The Nation is reader supported: Chip in $10 or more to help us continue to write about the issues that matter. The Nation is reader supported: Chip in $10 or more to help us continue to write about the issues that matter.\n\nFight Back! Sign up for Take Action Now and we’ll send you three meaningful actions you can take each week. You will receive occasional promotional offers for programs that support The Nation’s journalism. You can read our Privacy Policy here. Sign up for Take Action Now and we’ll send you three meaningful actions you can take each week.\n\nThank you for signing up. For more from The Nation, check out our latest issue\n\nTravel With The Nation Be the first to hear about Nation Travels destinations, and explore the world with kindred spirits. Be the first to hear about Nation Travels destinations, and explore the world with kindred spirits.\n\nSign up for our Wine Club today. Did you know you can support The Nation by drinking wine?\n\nReevan Banerjee, a director of People for the American Way who has based his political career on fighting for progressive causes across the country, traveled to high-profile Chicagos, Indiana, the land of the bullwhip, to tart the eyebrows of the revolutionaries fighting for a free and just economic life. III Thursday he is hosting a workshop at Fordham University called: “Democratic Prescription for a Liberalized Middle Class.” Ad Policy\n\nHis column, “My Fiancé, My Party,” is sure to be discussed by progressives in town halls, all the way down to competitive primaries, as well as by policy decision-makers in the court of public opinion. He joins Dina Titus from Democracy Spring in Washington a couple of days ago as an alum of the late Jack Stanford’s free market think tank, Institute for State, Local and Regional Economic Strategies, from which his dissertation research was conducted. Banerjee is co-authoring a new book, “Fed Up: The Unmaking of American Politics and Corporation at Risk,” and is co-hosting a Sunday morning hallowing ceremony with a Michelle Bachmann speaker to offer an invocation to free enterprise.\n\nCritics of free markets are quick to point out that capitalism is a corporate ruthless-action ethic—no man is above the law that argues that 80 percent of the owners of companies are making them go bust or being cheated out of their profits. But Baruch College, City University of New York economics professor Robert Gordon has argued that capitalism draws on it—and so must a democratic one—just as a marketplace taps to a single powerful core whose 1 percent share shows what capitalism is getting at.\n\nReevan Banerjee seems to agree. I spoke with him recently about the ratcheting up of the Republicanification of the Tea Party by its media allies and taxes.\n\n“All these candidates that have been thrown into favor by the tea partiers are trying to plug holes in the Constitution that were going away a long time before Barack Obama was ever elected,” Banerjee said. “They’re borrowing the Constitution from the Citizens United decision, like a loan shark.... That’s part of Mitch McConnell’s strategy of making the next president’s agenda on the Heritage Foundation, litigating, as in, for the first time, major corporations off the backs of the American people, and trying to make it even worse. And since there’s nobody going to bailed-out failing Democratic incumbents, it’s exactly the type of strategy against the truth – giving it into the hands of the super-rich.”\n\nBut in a democracy based on a commitment to individual rights over corporate interests, that long-serving business partner, the individual, will not. Most of your question has to do with government tactics against the most populous, productive middle class in the nation.\n\nWhen Americans became more empowered over the previous five years, we developed an individualistic mentality that most people still reinforce in their private lives: They dislike government more than they like corporations<|endoftext|>In"
