        "text": "<|endoftext|> — were matched up to 51% versus 38.7% for Bridgewater; Browns fans had an idea about the Bengals beating the Chiefs last week, but that hasn't happened for Ohio Stadium:\n\nThere at least some bias if you're a fan of the Bengals, folks. Cleveland will beat the Steelers in Week 12 if Ben Roethlisberger's surgically repaired shoulder doesn't heal by Week 4. Sure, you could order a bowl game for whoever wins the Browns and Steelers in the Ohio Stadium showdown, but I get beat down for my Bengals on a season-to-season basis. And let's be honest: I would go on every day to the set of a Big Bass Vongo Topping Show to hear every Bengals fan react to the 2016 playoff team…. When all else fails, I believe you'll dare to call that kind of self-mostvaluable loyalty.<|endoftext|>A story in the New York Daily News mentioned on April 12 that Northern Arizona University is coughing up millions of dollars in state funds “for a software program that gives cancer patients quotes to send to families.”\n\nThe program is provided by the Health Department of the federal Centers for Medicare & Medicaid (CMS) to stem the number of cancer patients choosing different cancer drugs by referring patients to another federal-medicine program that is currently a source of more than $500m a year in state and private aid.\n\nVaguely similar software\n\nBut the State of Arizona in January RTIe[1] terminated coverage of the newly launched Rare Care Trust dollar awarded to Northern Arizona University through a state and private initiative for information about specific cases of cancer. The state contract to use the research data “will not be fulfilled until at least 2016 [unless] a full state and federal class action suit seeks rectification.”\n\nThe State of Arizona in January Certified Care Provider Program Determinations Rejected\n\nThe only problem is, $249,000.\n\nUnveiled at the Q4 2016 Oral Questions to the Governor, the FY will not be resolved until June at least 2016. Just like with the Vaccine Injury Compensation Program of the US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), providers of vaccine injury relief and transfers cannot rely on private entities to resolve matters.\n\nDOI: 10.2588/JPH.38.1370\n\nBookmark with: Delicious\n\nDigg\n\nreddit\n\nFacebook\n\nStumbleUpon What are these? E-mail this to a friend Printable version<|endoftext|>By Kim Chol\n\nThe first public hearing was held on May 15 regarding a proposal at a government development agency to traffic in solar vehicles to alleviate the manmade electricity shortage at the Jiangnan Quarter, the southern Chinese township where I live.\n\n'It is important to rethink the way in which the public-private relationship works,' said Sara, a resident of the township. 'It is that kind of conflict that I dread in the area,' she added, referring to what the government has proposed to achieve 'virtually doubling the solar industry in the Lujiang-linan area.'\n\nWith plans for 50 turbines set to begin operation by 2020, the local wind farm owners have been vociferously promoting the use of technology to supply energy at less expensive cost to the industrial and commercial sectors of the region, despite the fact that solar and wind initially used to replace fossil-fuel power plants.\n\nFuel: A crane helps lift a new, centralized solar photovoltaic panel into a building\n\n'The houses of the growing number of solar energy entrepreneurs in the area must live under a regime of conservation because the only electricity that they receive is at the cost of astronomical rates generated by burning coal. By buying solar energy from the companies, we would totally pay the cost,' Garzi Zhang, chairman of the Shanghai Electric Company, told the hearing. REUTERS BLAZE / FILIPPO, NOEL\n\nIn Guangdong Province in the province's homeland north of the capital this week, strong opposition to charging certain amounts for solar modules was reported by residents.\n\n\"Solar electricity would increase electricity billups by about 60 percent,\" Zhang Man, general secretary of the Fujian Power Association, was quoted as saying during the hearing on May 15. He added that if the response from the solar industry in the Basantiang District was anything to go by, it would probably help curtail the decline in demand for electricity in other parts of China over the next three decades.\n\nStunned constituents of the nuclear industry in Hubei drowned in the spring of 2011 when the government responded to a petition calling for planning and legislation to regulate its operations and shut down the leaking parts of two reactor buildings. In Hubei city north of Chengdu, wind energy capacity has risen by more than 200 MW since 2010, Zhang claimed.\n\nAlthough Wind Power has only recently begun to attract some attention in Chinese cities, relations between Zhang, the wind industry, and the central government"
