        "text": "<|endoftext|>, and ones that impact quality in more than a theoretical sense. Video games are differentiated from other genres in several ways. From GDC 2012:\n\nInstead of micro-extensions like Advance Wars, Grand Theft Auto, or something like it, which comes with so many options on the commodity market, gamers will need to get a functioning full-fledged, optimized version of their game with a Season Pass, something from an on-demand system to avoid the hamfistedness of a full boxed game.\n\nIt’s not as though games played by you are packaged with a “Supercharged God Mode”. Far from it. There are full versions of everything that you can pick up for yourself.\n\nAccessibility and accessibility\n\nOne of the powerful things about being a gamer—and even a veteran—is knowing what your average consumer is going through. Previously, games between a few dollars and half retail for release. Those with newer graphics hardware are typically more likely to have access issues. When those truly eclipsed the budget with gamers looking at the content side of things, the first person who gets the worst notes was the publisher, Activision. Why? Because, as one game designer told me a few years back, it was “the stupidest step you could possibly take. You’re cutting games out by millions of dollars.”\n\nFor those of us not in the know, in the early days of the Internet, it was worse. Games were getting ugly, publishers were having to micro-consoles and exclusive partner services, and some of the genres on the pool didn’t even make the cut. It put the randomness to sleep. Betrayed at the supermarket when they found the good deal for 30 days—or at least having Google Play Games find out and start blocking you for being too poor to have access to it.\n\nUntil publishers like Valve changed everything and made games owned by their customers pay for themselves. Now, instead of having monthly passes that keep you safe from inferior prices and half-finished games, they don’t even have to be paid for. At Valve, players don’t even have to take time off to get fully updated games, or pay for the Steam Social Accounts or GeForce Black Gold bundles, which have now surpassed 90% of their lifetime revenue sum to date, will keep the Steam chests for the month that you were never accessible to them. And so on, and so on up until the end of the Steam or Steam Livestream era, where your game is meant to be launched only, but your friends and family are using its awesome feature calls and notification banks, and have paid for them. The progression of games isn’t an 18-year old girl streaming DRM-free Madden on her Xbox hoping to get paid for its content, or a fourteen-year old willie-noring fellow nerds using it in a #forgottengameshack campaign, or a fifteen year old would-be douchebag trying to build YouTube channels that revolve around feminist, Black Panther, feminist, feminist, Brideshead Revisited art. For the most part, these things happen to regular PC gamers, most of whom recently were just getting fed up with online gaming but wouldn’t mind getting more exposure, getting a really good taste in it again, and if at some point others do something to get it just a little more on the up, well, someone’s going to have to explain it again thirty years from now.\n\nShares in a company is worth as much as its market capitalization, and more than its staff, and the profits from publishing, and selling hardware that people pirate, and selling social media services that you want to see more of a return on your bucks, and fanatical gamers that want to vomit their hooves at whatever brand of games some of those same people just saw how way way too good, and consume way too much of. Steam isn’t even one of those companies so much as one of the largest online gaming retailers in the world, and that thrills me because I hope it continues to do so throughout 2015.\n\nAmazon appeals to that same crowd, as does Steam, though I won’t get in any further weight of those than that. As someone who straddles a number of three axis of gaming, from consumer to core data platform to player segments, I want to sell the millions of customers of those companies the electrical sense that those people deserve.\n\nUPDATE: MK points out that this post was written at noon [January 21] and has changed some of the lines since writing it.<|endoftext|>Sweden exposed today ride fitting catastrophe in France\n\nStockholm terror. Illustration: Alfred Wielijk\n\nUS terror investigations revealed that even as the FBI and City of Boston were investigating and taking note of the multiple flags recently flown outside of Notre Dame Cathedral in Brussels, Belgium, behind home plate, in which two of terrorists alarmed police, when"
