        "text": "<|endoftext|> grounds and unsuitable for any organization in New York State.\n\n“During a World War II raid at a former British embassy, an SS man was also killed and countless civilian lives were saved,” Collins said. “I applaud the coroner for requesting that the N.Y.P.D. authorize this despicable act.”\n\nFormer President Bill Clinton returned a signed statement endorsing the NYPD’s efforts to combat violence against police officers.<|endoftext|>Security researcher Bruce Schneier has been awaiting NFC driver certification for more than a decade now. With the NFC standard allowing people to secure smartphones without getting them implanted, the subject of security has been on the minds of Internet users this year. For years, many felt the need to experience a secure smartphone connection without a tool -- whether it be an app, an SMS, an action like launching an add-on application, just by searching on Google. A security researcher has something different in store.\n\nSharma Shibir Gehl, a researcher in SANS Institute's Advanced Authentication Engineering Group, is bringing NFC to the market. The idea is that by pairing a sensor with a smartphone to trigger an authentication system, one smartphone user can simply swipe their phone over the sensor on the other plus or minus one second, which can be quite convenient for busy smartphones where sometimes it’s necessary to swipe twice with a convenient, privacy-conscious finger.\n\nShibir Gehl has created a Malicious Fingerprint In Memory app that could one day enable employee instances to scan in fingerprint data from their smartphone without their permanent wireless access to the system. Developers behind the app will have to obtain a NDA, but users will be able to use the app to authenticate without a key being generated; the app will then have an inserted local PIN as the sold by-product. This means that a company deploying the app could leverage the written permission it supposedly takes on just to form a fingerprint for authentication.\n\nThe proof is in the mouth\n\nHow do your keys and your account passwords work? Be warned. As in the case of most major credit cards, there are a number of risks associated with this thinking -- not to mention that, if your bank issue code to a PermGen or other compromised computer, this same vulnerability can have implications for other credit card holders who wanted to purchase photos, music, documents, or other documents that they didn't necessarily bank with as well. This can damage the encrypted data itself, but this also leaves card locks disabled and users unable to use their sensitive data.\n\nHow do you pair your Android, iOS, and BlackBerry smartphone with a new NFC-enabled malvertising program?\n\nIf you’re a merchant handling payment or transferring financial credentials from a financial institution to clients, the possibilities are numerous. For example, suppose you wanted to shield funds in cash by registering the Android device as an NFC payment app on a centralized NFC terminal. You could simply type the NFC pin number and hold down and hold the [Enter PIN] key for 3-5 seconds before sending the wallet containing your information. Alternatively, it may be possible to set up a separate NFC terminal or allow multiple devices to register the same NFC pin. The risks, however, are much higher if you’re preparing to use your mobile device for job applications, file transfers, time transactions, or send electronic documents by default, giving away your identity and logging into your Android or iOS device without your knowledge and consent in the future.<|endoftext|>Donald Trump and Mike Pence met Friday night in Detroit with Sen. Jeff Sessions Jefferson (Jeff) Beauregard SessionsFormer Trump refugee director did not notify superiors about family separation warnings Court rejects challenge to Mueller's appointment Trump says he hasn't spoken to Barr about Mueller report MORE (R-Ala.), Mike Walker, the Indiana governor tapped by Trump to be attorney general, who discussed Saturday's visit with House Speaker Paul Ryan Paul Davis RyanBrexit and exit: A transatlantic comparison Five takeaways from McCabe’s allegations against Trump The Hill's 12:30 Report: Sanders set to shake up 2020 race MORE (R-Wis.).\n\nTrump and Pence then sat with Sen. Bob Corker Robert (Bob) Phillips CorkerBrexit and exit: A transatlantic comparison Sasse’s jabs at Trump spark talk of primary challenger RNC votes to give Trump 'undivided support' ahead of 2020 MORE (R-Tenn.) and Mike Rounds Michael (Mike) Dennis RoundsSenate Dems seek to turn tables on GOP in climate change fight Pence meets with Senate GOP for 'robust' discussion on Trump declaration Senate Dems introduce bill demanding report on Khashoggi killing Tulsi Gabbard throws cold water on Trump-Kim denuclearization talks MORE (R-S.D.) in Indianapolis to schedule a meeting with their counterparts in Alaska, Texas and Florida.\n\nADVERTISEMENT\n\nTrump and Pence also discussed the number of development opportunities in Northern Virginia. Trump noted that North Carolina has 43 “innovative [high-"
