        "text": "Debate on the state's tax policy remains high even as a General Assembly makes a move through committee on a measure aimed at rolling back the state's huge corporate tax deduction. Members of the General Assembly met with House Democrats on Tuesday to advance a Senate bill that would herald passage of a controversial tax policy measure that is meant to increase taxes on the wealthiest Americans. The Republican controlled Senate, however, is expected to vote on Wednesday on a controversial measure designed to roll back the state's corporate tax deduction. legislative aides said the measure, which would reduce the budget deficit by $2 billion, would prevent tax revenue from getting in the way of public spending. Senate Majority Leader Mitch McConnell, R-Ky., said advancing the measure through committee would be key to winning lawmakers' support. McConnell said 16 House Democrats had voted against the measure by Tuesday afternoon, short of the 48 Democrats who are up for re-election. Legislations may be finalized after that. Pennsylvania's tax deduction increases taxes on billionaires that don't control the state's economy, economists say. And legislative aides say the deduction could help stem the flow of public money that benefits the state's middle class. Republican efforts since then to restore the corporate tax deduction have come under fire from economists, who estimate the deduction could save Pennsylvania nearly $5 trillion in subsidies, tax revenue, and taxpayer spending when it goes into effect in May next year. Democrats, meanwhile, are concerned that ripping back the corporate deduction would lead to a slowing in economic growth. According to a report from the non-partisan and non-partisan Center for Budget Analysis and Policy, the state had a budget surplus of $80 billion over the next five years. House Democrats, who met Tuesday with McConnell, said the deduction was meant to increase taxes on the middle class and was meant to reduce taxes paid on high income earners. “We are deeply concerned that Pennsylvania’s corporate tax deduction will increase taxes on the middle class and put a substantial dent in the economic prosperity of the state for billionaires,\" Pennsylvania Department of Revenue spokesman Scott Stephenson told reporters. McConnell along with leaders from the Public Policy Alliance, a community-based group, decided in favor of ending the corporate tax deduction in 2012. The Pennsylvania Governors Association, which is lobbying for the legislation, volunteered to pass the Senate tax bill as it advanced to the state House of Representatives. Netflix founder David Francois has a past in French radio, but his early roles date back sometime to the early 1990s. \"I want to show people that this new world of hunked-off radio and TV is a farce in itself, and it's watched by millions of Americans today,\" Francois said. \"I need the right tools to tell a right story.\" The Lille-born French artist has been building his creative career on radio and TV for years. To do that, he hired animators and visuals for Ironman: Red Ramels, a series created by Netflix and directed by John Boswell. Francois passed the whole project on to Netflix in order to create graphics for the series. It's unclear if he intended to write \"Today the Knight,\" but he wouldn't say why he was drawn to Netflix. \"I tend to focus my work on radio and TV,\" Francois said. \"Usually because I haven't spent much time on other types of series.\" One of the biggest difficulties we encounter when it comes to smartphones is in deciding which new devices to introduce and which ones to appeal to consumers. smartphones like the Io and O4X traditionally come with multiple chips, so there is often no room for single chips. Netflix isn't in a position to let the Io or O4X enter the market, and while it's willing to let those types of devices out of the market, it never promises to support the traditional models. There's a wide range of gaming devices, like the Diaphon 980 and the O4X Plus. However, the O4X's two signature devices -- the HTC Pixel and HTC Pixel -- have been gaining traction for a long time, and Netflix hasn't tried to smooth out the mess since last year introducing its first major phone line with a single chip. But if you're smart enough to keep your cash elsewhere, you'll probably want to look to China where some of the most popular smartphones like the O4X and the IoX come with a single chip, and the recently released HTC Pixel. If you're cheap on those phones, you won't be deterred by the arrival of expensive devices like the Pixel, which comes with a 1.5GHz battery."
