        "text": "NEW IBUAKH, India — At least 57 people were injured when a large bomb exploded in Makaa state, chief of the central government's ministry of public safety, said Saturday. Flames erupted in the northwestern part of Kushawa, while explosions swept through the area. 12:41 p.m. EDT: A single-ton bomb exploded in Basat Mik state, officials said Saturday, killing 18 people and injuring 328 others. The explosions occurred along a halted village of Shulghelsabad, the ministry said. At 12:42 a.m., Indian officials said more than 10 homes — four couples and five children — were damaged in that area, read a press release. Several other homes were damaged as the explosions spread. The chief of the ministry of public safety of India said that explosions were concentrated in Mehnab village, 100 kilometres from the capital of Adinjaram district. At least 44 people were killed and 72 others injured by the large bomb, which hit a bridge crossing marking the New Year celebration in Quintana. It is not clear whether a deadly explosion caused the explosions. More than 95 people were injured and eight remain missing. Investigations are under way to determine a possible cause of the deaths. The explosion, which was concentrated throughout the region, occurred in a village about 90 miles north of Makaa state. The village witnessed a slew of smokerays and more than 72 flames erupted through the surrounding area, Indian authorities said. 12:45 p.m. EDT: At least 57 people were injured when an heavily-armed bomb exploded, officials said. At least nine others were injured when a single-ton bomb explode in Mehoutur village, 100 miles south of Kushawa. The explosions caused serious damage to the nearby communities, the region's interior minister Pratma Meenkar said. Timingpur, a small village near the road town of Timingpur, was severely damaged by the deadly blasts, said villagees officials. 12:55 a.m. EDT: At least 18 people and one child were injured when a large bomb exploded in Basat Mik state, police spokesmans and deputies said Saturday. Flames erupted along the road, according to the ministry of public safety of Greater India, the DIAH. Malaysian Interior Minister Sean Meenkar said the authorities are not doing nearly enough to stop the explosions, adding they are not doing enough \"to win the fight against terrorism.\" 12:15 a.m. EDT: At least 34 killed and 57 injured when a single-ton bomb exploded. The explosion hit a heavily-armed vehicle that was travelling outbound on the road, the DIAH said. Some 72 flames erupted in Timingpur, Adinjaram village and Mehoutur. Thirty-five killed and 57 injured among those, according to the Public Safety Authority in India (PMI). 12:50 a.m. EDT: Six people and one vehicle were injured Saturday when a bomb exploded in Makaa's Basat Mik state. The bomb was connected to a vehicle that was travelling on the road heading to a proposed bridge crossing in Quintana outside the state. Flames erupted along the road, fuelling tensions in the northwestern part of the country, the ministry said. Dozens of people were on the road when flames swept through nearby homes, according to the DIAH. During tensions with local residents, the roof where the bomb exploded was covered by smoke rays. Thirty-five killed and 60 injured, according to PMI. The roof where the single-ton bomb was covered with smoke rays and other debris. During the oblivion, five were killed, but no one is being detained. 12:15 a.m. EDT: At least 12 people and one child was injured when a large bomb exploded in Mehnab, government officials told reporters, in Makaa state's Adinjaram district. Thirty killed, 60 others injured and eight others remain missing, said the chief of the ministry of public safety of India's central government. Dozens were in other nearby villages, the official said. Greater Malaysian Interior Minister Pratma Vetnsab said the explosion had injured74 people in Makaa state. Authorities didn't say how many had died, but he said an investigation was ongoing. __"
