        "text": "\"We need to optimize ourselves in preparation for this offseason or the next game,\" King said. \"How can we optimize ourselves in this offseason? We have to go out and play hard. That's what this offseason is about. We have to go out playing hard.\" Senate GOP unveiled legislation to let 13 million purchase private health insurance (CNN) Senate Republican leader unveiled new legislation on Tuesday that would allow 13 million Americans of age 65 and older to buy private health insurance for the rest of the fiscal year and reduce insurance costs for the oldest families. The legislation, known as The Government Insurance Bill, reduces the cost of buying private health insurance by 25 percent over the same period. The bill would allow 13 million adults of age 65 and older to enroll in private health insurance plans for the rest of the fiscal year, according to the Congressional Budget Office. But Sen. Richard Burch (R-Ohio), the spear-head behind the bill, has called on an additional further 92 million adults of age 65 and older to enroll in private health insurance plans. \"We must reduce costly insurance costs for the oldest families,\" Burch said on Tuesday. UPDATE: Sen. Richard Burch (R-KY), the spearhead behind the bill, has called on Americans to register their plans before the fiscal year. Burch's bill would reduce the cost of private health insurance plans for those under 65 by 25 percent. It would also introduce a one-year run-of-the-wheels expansion of domestic violence prevention and substance abuse treatment programs that begins every two years. At the same time, the bill would allow 13 million adults age 65 and older to enroll in private and public health insurance. It would also allow an additional 92 million oldest families to sign up for their registration. The bill also introduces a one-year expansion of private health insurance programs for those under 65 that begins every two years, saving up $550 million from the previous year. It is not known when the expansion will be completed across all four regions. Valve releases Call of Duty for PC and mobile on April 31st, 2017 By Nintendo Effects Staff Valve has announced the release of a new Call of Duty game for the Wii Xbox One. Call of Duty 2 is an acclaimed remake of the Nintendo Effects title Call of Duty offering players a first-person tour of the region's ancient deserts alongside a renaissance of heroes based off of the original Call of Duty. For PC and mobile, Call of Duty prepares a dedicated team of combatant forces to set out through an array of conflicts to deal with the threat of a conflict. Your team embarks on a powerful and heroic campaign to liberate the region's ancient desert in a revealing remake of Valve's highly acclaimed title Call of Duty. The game, similar to a Nintendo Effects remake, re-imagines the landscape of the region's deserts in very viscerating detail, illuminating the combination of heroism and extreme survival necessary to fight against a force of darkness. Your team is sent on a mission to free a trio of Pymnocdiae warriors while players embark on a powerful mission to assassinate humanity. Call of Duty 2 offers real-time navigation of the region on a remotely navigable map and can be downloaded from COVERTH for PC or mobile. Players will explore the region's once-dominant deserts and be driven through vast trenches, caves, environments and caves, where they'll be able to collect a variety of items and activate an unprecedented array of powers. Teams preparing for the renaissance of the region's ancient deserts will also be eligible for the opportunity. Valve has previously worked titles such as Nintendo Effects, Call of Duty: Age of Darkness and Call of Duty on movies such as Guardians of the Galaxy and Star Crush as well as Infinity Rings and Aquincer. Players over a certain age have a chance to take part in Call of Duty's acclaimed sequel trilogy, Star Crush, where players are sent on a powerful journey through sprawling trenches, while your team must face off in a powerful conflict. Call of Duty 2 will be released later this month on both PC and mobile, specific instances on the PS4/ Xbox One and Windows platforms. As of writing, the game currently has a pre-order price tag of US$49.99. Valve Gaming's team is working to make available the Call of Duty 2 post-launch update as soon as today. For details about the update, you can follow Call of Duty on Facebook, Twitter @npsi_gamernews and other news sources."
