        "id": "s-9017df0ccaf3802141af618e",
        "sourceId": 1023,
        "text": "Witnesses said tens thousands were killed as Israeli forces unhurled tanks and grenades. Civils were battling to gain control over Alam Shaada, a residential community in an area of between 100,000 and 20,000 Palestinians. Alam Shaada, which once owned two Palestinian houses, was reclaimed last month after Israeli airstrikes hit 16 Israeli soldiers, who killed Palestinians and injured more than 500 others. Witnesses have said the area showed no sign of fighting. Yusef Ginsim, a factory worker who lives in Jamor, said: \"We heard gunshots as the Israeli army approached the area. When we got to that spot we saw assailants throwing stones at us.\" Jerusalem's woman, Merufa, who is a 22-year-old factory worker, was traversing the opening of the Alam Shem Bridge, a shopping mall south of the city of Jamor, when a missile swarmed into her. Then a series of massive explosions broke out near Merufa's office and next to her home. She said: \"By the time there was a shortage of food. People stopped going shopping. It became very dark. \"We grew unable to afford food.\" Shad Yunallah, a witness from Gaza, said most of the people hit were rural people. He saw assailants walking down sidewalks and then abduct them when they arrived in Israel. \"We saw so many residents of Jamor die in an escalating air strike on Israel,\" he said. \"We heard gunshots from residents. I'm not entirely clear on that yet. \"Do we know how many people died? \"We saw people being wounded, not shot.\" The third witness, a 27-year-old Palestinian resident of Gaza, said he and others were hit by encircled Israeli forces near the city of Jamor. Mohammed Hadat, a 26-year-old resident of Hatsan, said she first saw soldiers attack her home just south of the city of Jamor. \"They attacked our shop and then smashed the window behind my house.\" She said she then saw Israeli troops fighting down the road to her home. She said: \"I saw two soldiers lying on the roof of my house on the road and were spotting stones at me. I was taken to local hospital in critical condition.\" Farsan Jahid, a resident of Hatsan, said he and his family were on a trip to Israel to stay with his family. \"When they arrived, we witnessed two soldiers break up our site and storming the windows, planting their batons on the windows of my family's house.\" Mr Jahid said forces stormed the neighbourhood, killing 21 Palestinians and injuring 25 other people. About half of them were in critical condition. But he said there were voices of support from the families of those injured. \"Fortunately, many homes in this neighbourhood were destroyed and those soldiers and their families managed to defend themselves,\" he said. \"We are taking their case to a military court.\" The Israeli Defence Ministry has been conducting air strikes to capture hundreds of the injured. Al-Malhalem mayor Mussim Natar said that Israeli forces would conduct a night airstri to restore the conditions of all those injured. \"We are also looking after the conditions of all those injured. Israeli forces are carrying out an evening airstri,\" he said. Fighter bombs encircled neighbourhood of Alam Shaada At least 10 people were injured when fighter bombs encircled the neighbourhood of Alam Shaada on Sunday, breaking down a complex that is now Israel's largest building, Defence Ministry spokesman Barry Walker said. \"Ten people were injured in the destruction of the residential complex and over 10 people were hospital in critical condition. Many of them were children,\" he added. How many were hurt? Mr Walker said it is not known how many people were injured. Sunday's highly intense chemical attack swarmed the city centre of Jerusalem into the dark, impoverising vast parts of the city and forcing dozens of people to leave, he said. \"The bombers stopped our bus, attacked our shops, narrowly targeted our homes,\" he said. It broke down a residential complex along Alam Shem Bridge also today. Onlookers heard voices of support coming from the ground floor of the building. \"As we accessed that spot we heard a wave of gunshots coming from the left floor of the window of the residential complex,\" Defence Ministry spokesman Mustafaam Khamenael Ahmad said."
