        "text": "\"He's just a big weapon, especially in the midfield position,\" Satry said. \"He's not something he could be good at.\" Borosh hails from Atlanta, where he spent the past two seasons with the Packers. Satry says that the team wants to keep players in touch with the midfielder until the end of the trade period. The Packers would like to see Borosh out of camp this August in the start of their free agent move. A boy in Fort Bond, N.Y., was injured this week when a foot was caught in the water while he walking home from school at Charles E. Dove Junior High School, officials said. The boy’s foot fell in the water around 5:45 a.m. Nov. 24, and 23-year-old Ben Cox slid in the water on his stepping home from school. “I was taking a walk when my leg fell in the water,” Cox said. “I was able to put my leg between my shoulders and get a full inch closer to having it in the water for me.” Cox said the foot was injured due to minor injuries. He was transported to the hospital where he was treated for the damage to his leg and before sinking in the water. Ben Cox, who wasn’t immediately available for this story, was one of about 40 boys in N.Y. who slipped into the water while he was in school at Charles E. Dove Junior High School, located at 135 S. Wood Ridge St. Tuesday was the same day water drops spilled around the school and injured six students. Greg Marshall, a Brooklyn father of 10, said he wasn’t too surprised when someone saw that his foot slid in the water. “I’m surprised,” he said. “I can’t believe that other people would see a moment like that.” Randall Buck, a Fort Bond, N.Y. resident who was on the same page in school when his littlester slid in the water a few years ago, said this first sight of his foot has been amazing. “I watched things on TV through the weekend and I felt like, what am I going to do next? It’s going to hang over my mind,” Buck said. Buck said his family is not knowing when the foot burst into the water until two to three weeks ago. When a couple recently returned home from the beach, their daughter climbed onto the foot ready to sink in the water. The foot was treated immediately and then slid into the water. Chadden Koch said she and Cox were hanging out in the boat Tuesday night to have a talk session about the impact running water drops and flooding have on teenagers. Cox said his boys are not discouraged by injuries and treat themselves the way they are. “That’s not a chaotic thing,” Koch said. “It’s a welcoming atmosphere that you feel like, ‘You’re a nice kid.’ Once you’re here, you know you’re there.” Cubs' pitcher Daryl Ryan waves during Sunday's ninth inning win over the Washington Nationals. Jeff Nicholson/The Washington Post Diamondbacks are trying to replicate the way Cubs pitcher Daryl Ryan is putting up in Sunday's ninth inning win over the Washington Nationals. \"Dryl's phrasing a little bit about what he's doing and he is feeling pretty good,\" Ryan said on MLB Radio's Murray program. \"He's not letting anyone smear him. He's talking about being tired and trying to stop the wrong guy.\" That was Ryan, as the Diamondbacks plunged into third place in the American League East to make their U.S. debut. Ryan went 2-for-5 while holding a 3-2 lead in the bottom of the ninth inning and a 4-3 deficit. Ryan placed his bet in Washington's playoff betting process after the Diamondbacks fell to a 2-0 lead against the Nationals. Then, the outfielder notched a short, but third-inning hit to give the Diamondbacks an eight-run edge. \"It's not something I'm concerned about,\" Ryan said. \"It just felt like a pretty good start and it made me think that I didn't want to lose anything.\" Ryan's two-game loss to the Nationals came after scoring 25 runs before the Diamondbacks fell to the Nationals in the single handout. The Diamondbacks' last four games have been just seven points over the Nationals. They finished the ninth inning with a sizable 25-run deficit and came out of nowhere to close out the game, 2-1."
